#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125663} :activity/id #uuid "54746d4e-16f7-5527-9c6f-def91a1f4b6e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049828 :topic/id #uuid "171bc42f-1e67-52f7-92c6-e9af227f8d0f" :topic/math-academy-id 1882} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124391 :question/id #uuid "59873e3c-c8ba-5844-874a-01cf298b361d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7992"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "713fb1a0-5d7b-5cde-aafe-de5887086e1b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562482}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "e12eeac1-466a-5f3e-b414-d25fd0ed1c89" :step/math-academy-id 29837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125512 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124393 :question/id #uuid "d6a69eb7-e598-51e8-92e9-fad42b3c0978" :question/math-academy-id "e-8027"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56d457de-8511-5870-98e3-b81e72e60585" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570755}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "e7d9de02-5d84-5776-9397-cf6b426b9eb4" :step/math-academy-id 29974 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125846 :tutorial/content "The range of a quadratic function depends on the direction in which its graph opens. Regardless of whether the equation is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c,\n$$\n\nvertex form, \n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k,\n$$\n\nor factored form,\n\n$$\ny = a(x-x_1)(x-x_2),\n$$\n\nit opens upwards if the leading coefficient $a$ is positive and downwards if $a$ is negative.\n\nNow, consider a parabola that opens upwards ($a>0$). In this case, the minimum output corresponds to the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, while there is no maximum output. Therefore, if the vertex is $(h,k),$ then the range is $y \\geq k.$\n\nFor example, in the graph below, the $y$-value of the vertex is $y=-2$, so the range of the function is $y \\geq -2.$\n\n![](images/ac/ac254aaa0bdab2999e07c46f82becd37fb4b8ee6e816ad91842d6e750eb90b86.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "505edbed-bec8-50a0-8a52-67e25a936784" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5155 :tutorial/title "The Range of an Upward-Opening Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "cd9056e9-1e4b-5477-b146-89ad0b007f19" :step/math-academy-id 32412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125716 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will discuss the properties of quadratic functions. Specifically, we learn how to determine the domain and range of a quadratic function.\n\nRemember that a quadratic function takes the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = ax^2 + bx + c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nThe first thing to note is that the domain of *any* quadratic function is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ because it is defined for all values of $x.$\n\nDetermining the range of a quadratic function is slightly more involved, as we'll see shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "754107de-231b-53d3-9c87-0c63f43c0733" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1649 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "382f3a3b-5fd1-5d9c-acfc-d0673b1bdd60" :step/math-academy-id 4602 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124225 :question/id #uuid "d2479726-b486-5bc2-864f-98a3659d81d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-1470"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bc2396-f949-57a0-be50-d5606abe3372" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048398}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570780}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parabola Given in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "967be501-fa9c-5999-83c9-e020f0c1112d" :step/math-academy-id 4604 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125666}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125717 :tutorial/content "Now consider a parabola that opens downwards ($a<0$). In this case, there is no minimum output, but the maximum output corresponds to the $y$-coordinate of the vertex. Therefore, if the vertex is $(h,k),$ then the range is $y \\leq k.$\n\nFor example, in the graph below, the $y$-value of the vertex is $y=2$, so the range of the function is $y \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4390b9cec5d5fce67691421479151be3592967f95d22ddde307c6d56c630879.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "886809b2-0204-52ae-8f7b-c83f91d56f41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1651 :tutorial/title "The Range of a Downward-Opening Parabola"} :step/id #uuid "0db717c3-7b6a-5b97-8fd1-af369e6ff4d6" :step/math-academy-id 4609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125512}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124226 :question/id #uuid "f69335f5-b5df-5053-8127-a7489c711b5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1472"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb0d6e0e-bf61-5fe9-bf8a-ab15c5846e11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050577}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570791}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parabola Given in Factored Form"} :step/id #uuid "87f0d6e4-1968-5bc6-bd74-da8ebb01d739" :step/math-academy-id 4610}] :activity/title "Domain and Range of Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124689}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561069} :activity/id #uuid "cd1774d0-cc54-52b2-8fb3-b23040b59b85" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049828 :topic/id #uuid "171bc42f-1e67-52f7-92c6-e9af227f8d0f" :topic/math-academy-id 1882} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e9b9ea-0207-5775-8719-53fb983a237d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-4,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560403} {:answer/id #uuid "4af5a9b7-e5a9-5f98-ab91-fd32e53abb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560528} {:answer/id #uuid "6afc1ce6-29e6-52f3-a10c-5b118b05a8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-12]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560589} {:answer/id #uuid "a63d5147-8df3-58cb-814c-76bc9d5b75c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,12]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560701} {:answer/id #uuid "c627679c-f4b5-5c2f-a981-1b2c19fcb88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560701} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ddf9e1a-4d8f-52d7-a6b4-217f0d19f0e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ffbeb0a-9779-5c35-a530-341343c5a9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-90970" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}-8x-4?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0,$ $b=-8,$ and $c=-4.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-8)}{2\\cdot (-1)}=-4.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-4$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-8x-4 \\\\  & =-(-4)^{2}-8(-4)-4 \\\\  & =-16+32-4 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-4,12),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,12].$\n\n![](images/52/52d836e66719861c2fff226f5ae8d38485d3874b51365dddf8e3d3e99088972c.png)"} :step/id #uuid "294ef667-0d61-5f95-93c6-77309e10d176" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "332e1d80-7be9-5c06-af32-c685cf22b488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560478} {:answer/id #uuid "66edb060-6c48-5d86-bc94-ca07b3f6eff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560580} {:answer/id #uuid "7014d6a2-28c0-585c-9c3a-7254efaca4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560602} {:answer/id #uuid "9bbe7726-805f-5ecb-9261-1dd23f6ff79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560682} {:answer/id #uuid "c24bd33a-9854-52b8-8040-811faade6cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{4},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560580} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e3ba704-088a-57fb-bc54-70440ac77e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "723435c8-f6c2-5011-8005-6ac70ede7884" :question/math-academy-id "q-90983" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-5(x-1)(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-5<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+2}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5(x-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(\\frac{3}{2}-2) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{4}),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}].$\n\n![](images/42/426a8277921ca2c8282567eeb076c3dcf1f0f1c729c4ebd2e5d624f51b78d61e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "459a4996-3ca0-5e5e-9a1f-8d2cd29c20c6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4876c102-2429-5e92-adbc-25362d13d71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560524} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c74505d-9ba7-507a-b46d-ccacd493ae26" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51afc4aa-5aa7-5db1-9827-415340924584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560542} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d40415-4aca-5a00-a88c-3b8ead6018b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560874} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82d46fe3-a01c-51f2-bb08-72a3443e001a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560640} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c25361-0058-5188-b424-05769d00535f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "baf92e1a-9022-5719-8869-44081e0988df" :question/math-academy-id "q-250613" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-(x+6)^{2}-10.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-6$ and $k=-10,$ the vertex is $(-6,-10).$\n\n- Since $a=-1<0,$ the parabola opens downwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens downwards, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $(-\\infty ,-10],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le -10.$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7aa50770fd1c24866ca5d5a8df1b04dc7260b4a5b346f8296757c59d33ba545b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "97fce570-7fc4-5f25-8fc4-16001b69205d"}] :activity/title "Domain and Range of Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560357}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570832} :activity/id #uuid "5985754e-714a-5c2c-b300-e0ee4263e5d0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049828 :topic/id #uuid "171bc42f-1e67-52f7-92c6-e9af227f8d0f" :topic/math-academy-id 1882} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eeca0e1-b6de-52ef-a755-30e10b649fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570227} :answer-field/id #uuid "16033fcf-6425-52d7-842c-b314f27cbd64" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570585} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991ba2b0-831a-53eb-b183-63a7aa87cc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570395} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a33357-db32-588d-8f62-a8e54ad0fed1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66d81624-7ec5-5cda-a1be-d41d2b795a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570304} :answer-field/id #uuid "53804295-71ff-5921-a1f0-85877fe7f459" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb786a48-6efc-557e-bdac-ba3e5bfceb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-250655" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-(x+7)(x+9).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-9$ and ${x}_{2}=-7,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-9-7}{2}=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-8$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(x+7)(x+9) \\\\  & =-(-8+7)(-8+9) \\\\  & =-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-8,1),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 1.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cb245de80be7125a1e8874781ed21fc2845f25f7ee8e1e43356819f87fe1b6c.png)"} :step/id #uuid "30cad7c6-2bfe-57a8-8544-4f6a46441b2e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f449186-68af-505d-8ba1-e89490d3588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570323} {:answer/id #uuid "812ca881-ad52-5c82-8b1a-ad9ec2c3192f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-7,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570353} {:answer/id #uuid "892d7645-442d-5314-b45a-7ea9f816363b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570366} {:answer/id #uuid "97d84504-9c16-5ada-9158-d437803d7880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570393} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf18488-82e6-55f7-bc88-70816d80833f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-7]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570513} :answer-field/id #uuid "e59cc3b3-2d1b-5a47-a0da-cf1c94d9ece9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a71e9cb-8bb6-5c17-8cab-a2b0b8515a29" :question/math-academy-id "q-90979" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-3(x+1)^{2}-7?$" :question/worked-solution "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-1$ and $k=-7,$ the vertex is $(-1,-7).$\n\n- Since $a=-3<0,$ the parabola opens downwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens downwards, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output. \n\nTherefore, the range is $(-\\infty ,-7].$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/21/21049fc3aa8d6e2b64c4fd5db723c00e5878473ad8b96f25271c46edc48ed3ea.png)"} :step/id #uuid "665ef093-e5bb-5ba9-9e9a-d396adc7f6c4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df9a543-b5df-589d-9923-b17fb9588714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549338} {:answer/id #uuid "3d610a34-2b2c-5b7f-a6ee-1208ff230466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549439} {:answer/id #uuid "5d61e674-449f-5c1e-8470-6d7a912ecbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549503} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1dd293-3094-5184-81d3-8f43b189007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549619} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f407d3-59e6-5e5d-8e6f-2e775ce3251f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549619} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f8e4aed-0ad7-583b-bf1d-eb2a74efae0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7bfd7586-e0f5-50a2-a9b8-3d8eb173ed99" :question/math-academy-id "q-35155" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}(x+2)(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-\\frac{1}{3}<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-2+2}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=0$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x+2)(x-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0+2)(0-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(0,\\frac{4}{3}),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{4}{3}].$\n\n![](images/d9/d9c96e13ab078005333cdf02d8939c96d41fe6802b7f4caf0d4bab041cf8a314.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7095dfca-d543-5340-966b-1a6ee03b8d50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b63033a-24e2-5903-b295-91cc93bb9990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,13]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570256} {:answer/id #uuid "9b259075-8cb8-5df3-945a-7a9c6ac033fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,14]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570398} {:answer/id #uuid "dc78e2b3-11f1-573d-b784-24f309474acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[13,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570510} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf2fb45-750e-5cdd-afa7-44f40ee5659d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[14,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570535} {:answer/id #uuid "f1daca14-4c4e-530e-9222-4e3f4ba8d119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[13,14]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570398} :answer-field/id #uuid "27b48aea-ee27-5772-a6cc-4c2cfba244bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87522f6e-547f-5501-803f-38e69a4ab8bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-106283" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-2(x+13)^{2}+14?$" :question/worked-solution "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-13$ and $k=14,$ the vertex is $(-13,14).$\n\n- Since $a=-2<0,$ the parabola opens downwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens downwards, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output. \n\nTherefore, the range is $(-\\infty ,14].$\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/08/08b2ebf2fb3ecb8e02eed81d47e349dad22bde0d97e8920fabc75a8e2301da2b.png)"} :step/id #uuid "a0240a55-8160-5d0a-b1e1-609611199c9d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403b07c6-f056-5c1e-9ced-36e0c1b0f3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570231} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc0af6-8ea4-53b5-b553-6a9490590d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570293} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2b3537-03e7-553c-8206-65bd50cf13a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570312} {:answer/id #uuid "cf772beb-dd46-5ca1-b434-51819c40e211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570485} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e05d0-d612-5386-a5a0-a3bf0dde0b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570231} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e87ba1c-6874-5d0a-996a-3c1acc628a9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e645c316-71e4-58c2-8dda-54dfd003d3ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-90968" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+6x-8?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0,$ $b=6,$ and $c=-8.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{6}{2\\cdot (-1)}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=3$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =-(3)^{2}+6(3)-8 \\\\  & =-9+18-8 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(3,1),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1].$\n\n![](images/36/362626b55b357f91efb0fc89a51361bd8a9be368a6645becd3db07580accaaff.png)"} :step/id #uuid "b221606f-e40b-54aa-b16e-e307f3403f69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570827}}] :activity/title "Domain and Range of Quadratic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570103}]}
